<header><p class="st-kicker">principality · Poland, Lithuania and Russia</p><h1 class="st-title">Principality of Minsk</h1><p class="st-meta">1101 CE – 1326 CE (approx.) · capital Minsk</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A small principality of the Polotsk lands whose town grew into the capital of modern Belarus.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/minsk">Minsk</a> first appears in the chronicle in 1067, when the sons of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yaroslav-the-wise">Yaroslav the Wise</a> sacked it during their war with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vseslav-of-polotsk">Vseslav of Polotsk</a> and defeated him on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-on-the-nemiga-river">Nemiga</a> river. After Vseslav died in 1101 his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gleb-vseslavich">Gleb</a> received Minsk, which became a separate principality.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gleb-vseslavich">Gleb Vseslavich</a> made <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/minsk">Minsk</a> a power in its own right and fought <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kyiv">Kiev</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vladimir-ii-monomakh">Vladimir Monomakh</a> until he was captured and died in Kiev in 1119. Afterwards the principality was a minor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/appanage">appanage</a> of the Polotsk dynasty. Little is known of its later rulers or institutions, and it lived in the shadow of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/polotsk">Polotsk</a> and the growing <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/grand-duchy-of-lithuania">Grand Duchy of Lithuania</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>By the early 14th century <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/minsk">Minsk</a> was dependent on <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/grand-duchy-of-lithuania">Lithuania</a>, and around 1326 a Prince Vasily of Minsk appears as a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassal</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gediminas">Gediminas</a>. It was absorbed into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/grand-duchy-of-lithuania">Grand Duchy of Lithuania</a>.</p><p class="st-conf">Few sources survive for this state; details are uncertain.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/principality-of-minsk.jpg" alt="Principality of Minsk" width="320" height="434"><figcaption>Image: Чаховіч Уладзіслаў · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%D0%9F%D0%BE%D0%BB%D0%B0%D1%86%D0%BA%D0%B0%D1%8F_%D0%B7%D1%8F%D0%BC%D0%BB%D1%8F_%D1%9E_%D0%B2%D0%B0%D0%B5%D0%BD%D0%BD%D0%B0-%D0%BF%D0%B0%D0%BB%D1%96%D1%82%D1%8B%D1%87%D0%BD%D1%8B%D1%85_%D0%BF%D0%B0%D0%B4%D0%B7%D0%B5%D1%8F%D1%85_XII_%D1%81%D1%82.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1101 CE – 1326 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/12th-century">12th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/14th-century">14th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>principality</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Minsk</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Gleb Vseslavich</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Eastern Orthodox Christianity</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Old East Slavic</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/belarus">Belarus</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Mensk Principality</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1214&amp;focus=principality-of-minsk" aria-label="Principality of Minsk on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5291 327 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Principality of Minsk lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5611 583L5630 540M5611 583L5556 562"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5630 540h0M5556 562h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5611 583h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5611 583h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Minsk. <a href="/atlas?year=1214&amp;focus=principality-of-minsk">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
